    Ignore eas.json in the runtime fingerprint, add a full review tripwire
    
    The runtime fingerprint is the runtimeVersion, so it should move only when a
    new native build is genuinely required. eas.json was hashed whole-file, so a
    feature-flag URL tweak in its EXPO_PUBLIC_* env (PR #8990) drifted all six
    fingerprints and broke the check on develop — even though that env is inlined
    into the JS bundle and delivered via OTA, needing no rebuild. Add eas.json to
    .fingerprintignore so OTA-safe env changes no longer bump the runtimeVersion or
    trigger store builds, and regenerate the runtime fingerprints.
    
    To keep visibility of such changes, add a second fingerprint, fingerprints.full,
    as a review tripwire: it hashes the native-adjacent inputs the runtime
    fingerprint deliberately waves through (eas.json, app version, build scripts,
    .gitignore), skipping only the per-commit `extra` section so it doesn't churn
    every commit. A full-only mismatch means an OTA-safe input moved — review it and
    regenerate to acknowledge; no store build is due. The runtime fingerprint stays
    byte-identical to what EAS computes (still via the expo-updates CLI); the full
    one is computed via @expo/fingerprint with eas.json folded back in (it's nulled
    by .fingerprintignore). @expo/fingerprint is now a direct devDependency.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>

