    Add GrowthBook-controlled Pyroscope continuous profiling
    
    Wires the backend into the Pyroscope server (Couchers-org/tools#36) via the
    pyroscope-io in-process agent, controlled entirely by GrowthBook flags so
    profiling can be turned on/off and retuned in prod without a redeploy:
    
      - profiling_enabled      (bool)   master on/off (default off)
      - profiling_sample_rate  (int)    samples/sec (default 20, clamped 1-250)
      - profiling_mode         (string) "wall" or "cpu" (default wall)
    
    Each profiled process (the 4 API workers + the background-job workers; the
    scheduler is skipped) starts its own reconcile thread after
    setup_experimentation(); it polls the flags every 30s and (re)configures or
    shuts the agent down to match. The agent is per-process since it can't be
    inherited across the forkserver boundary.
    
    Two non-obvious constraints, both verified empirically against pyroscope-io
    1.0.9 on Python 3.14:
      - The agent uses py-spy, which needs CAP_SYS_PTRACE; without it it silently
        samples nothing. Added cap_add: SYS_PTRACE to the backend service.
      - enable_logging must never be True on a re-configure: the Rust global logger
        can only be initialised once, and a second attempt is a non-unwinding panic
        that aborts the process. We pass enable_logging=False unconditionally.
    
    Bearer auth to the nginx ingest gate goes via http_headers (the agent has no
    auth_token option). Profiling is off unless PYROSCOPE_SERVER is set, so dev and
    test are unaffected.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

