    Optimize moderation_state_column_visible to avoid full table scans
    
    The old implementation used 4 EXISTS subqueries that each joined a content
    table (host_requests, group_chats, friend_relationships, event_occurrences)
    with moderation_states via moderation_state_id. PostgreSQL converted these
    into hashed SubPlans that eagerly seq-scanned all 4 content tables plus
    moderation_states (4x), totaling ~580k rows scanned — even when only 20
    notifications were needed due to LIMIT.
    
    The new implementation looks up moderation_states directly by PK and checks
    visibility there. For visible/unlisted, no content table access is needed.
    For the rare shadowed case, it uses object_type + object_id on the
    moderation_state row to do a targeted PK lookup into just the relevant
    content table.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.6 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

